/-!
# Wreath products, regularly over `ℚ(T)`

Every finite semiabelian group is a regular Galois group over `ℚ(T)`.  A semiabelian group is one
that can be built from the trivial group by repeatedly passing to a quotient of a wreath product
`A ≀ᵣ H` by a finite abelian group `A`; the group theory of that induction lives in
`InverseGalois/Solvable/`, which reduces the whole class to the single geometric statement proved
here: **if `H` is a regular Galois group over `ℚ(T)` and `C` is finite cyclic, then so is
`C ≀ᵣ H`**.

The construction is the one of Dentzer and Stoll.  Take a regular realization `E / ℚ(T)` of `H` and
a primitive element `θ` of it, transcendental over `ℚ`; its conjugates `h(θ)`, one for each `h ∈ H`,
are `m = |H|` different coordinates on the same curve.  Take a regular cyclic realization `N / ℚ(T)`
of `C`, and pull it back along each of the `m` substitutions `T ↦ h(θ) + c`.  The compositum `M` of
`E` with the `m` pullbacks carries an action of `H` permuting the layers, so its Galois group embeds
into the wreath product `C ≀ᵣ H`; the theorem is that for all but finitely many intercepts `c` the
embedding is onto, which is a statement about degrees, and the degree is computed by Kummer theory
after extending the constants to `ℚ̄`.

## The geometric side

Over `ℚ̄` a cyclic extension of the line is radical, so each layer is generated by an `n`-th root of
a product `∏ (T - tᵢ) ^ eᵢ`, and the pullback of the layer along `T ↦ h(θ) + c` is generated by an
`n`-th root of the same product evaluated at `h(θ) + c`.  The compositum of the layers therefore has
the maximal degree `n ^ m` over the geometric base exactly when those `m` radicands are independent
modulo `n`-th powers, and independence is detected place by place: it suffices that each conjugate
have a place at which it, and no other conjugate, meets one of the points `tᵢ`.  A translation
`θ ↦ θ + c` moves the conjugates past each other, so all but finitely many intercepts work.

* `RET.Wreath.LineMap`, `RET.Wreath.ParamFinite` — a coordinate on a curve presents it as a finite
  extension of the line, and any two coordinates present the same one.
* `RET.Wreath.ThetaSimple`, `RET.Wreath.PlaceLocal`, `RET.Wreath.PrivatePlace` — almost every value
  of a coordinate is taken simply, and almost every translation gives every conjugate a place at
  which it alone meets a branch point.
* `RET.Wreath.RadicandOrd`, `RET.Wreath.Independence`, `RET.Wreath.RadicandIndep` — the order of a
  radicand at such a place, and the resulting independence of the family modulo `n`-th powers.
* `RET.Wreath.CyclicNormalForm` — the exponents of a multi-point Kummer model can be taken coprime
  to the degree, which is what makes the orders visible.
* `RET.Wreath.KummerPinch` — adjoining independent radicals multiplies the degree by `n ^ m`, and
  does so minimally.

## The arithmetic side

The layers have to be produced inside one fixed algebraic closure of `ℚ(T)`, and their degrees have
to be compared over `ℚ̄(T)` rather than over `ℚ(T)`, because that is where the radicals live.  The
dictionary between the two is regularity: a finite extension of `ℚ(T)` is regular exactly when
extending its constants to `ℚ̄` does not drop its degree.

* `RET.Wreath.Conjugates` — the data of a conjugate-layer construction, the compositum `M`, and the
  embedding of its Galois group into the wreath product.
* `RET.Wreath.GeomBase`, `RET.Wreath.CompositumDegree`, `RET.Wreath.GeomDegree`,
  `RET.Wreath.GeomNormal` — the geometric closure of an intermediate field, its degree, and the
  fact that extension of constants preserves normality, Galois-ness and cyclicity.
* `RET.Wreath.ConjPrimitive`, `RET.Wreath.ConjSep` — a transcendental primitive element of a regular
  extension has conjugates no two of which differ by a constant.
* `RET.Wreath.Layers`, `RET.Wreath.SubstHom`, `RET.Wreath.LayerHom` — substituting a conjugate for
  the variable, and extending the substitution to a layer without leaving the compositum.
* `RET.Wreath.KummerLayer` — the radical generator of a cyclic geometric layer.
* `RET.Wreath.Intercept` — the choice of a rational intercept avoiding the exceptional set.

## Main results

* `Rigidity.RET.Wreath.isRegularInverseGalois_wreath_cyclic` — the wreath product of a finite cyclic
  group by a regular Galois group over `ℚ(T)` is again one.
* `IsRegularInverseGalois.wreath` — the same for a wreath product by any finite abelian group.
* `isRegularInverseGalois_of_isSemiabelian` — every finite semiabelian group is a regular Galois
  group over `ℚ(T)`.
* `RET.Wreath.SmallGroups` — the entries of the catalogue that the group-theoretic criteria for
  semiabelianness supply: every finite group all of whose Sylow subgroups are cyclic, every group
  of squarefree order, and every group of order `p`, `p ^ 2`, `p ^ 3` or `p * q`.
-/
